//? Title: DDIM: Respaced, Clipping-Consistent Reverse Paths
//? Subtitle: Native tensor components with editable training and sampling loops
//? Requires: ddpm.mepml
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
This report preserves the original ddim experiment and moves reusable tensor operations into datamunge. Parameter stores, objectives, minibatch loops, optimizer writes and sampling trajectories are visible in the complete program.
)
\toc

> Method, preserved protocol and observed limitations

[Song, Meng and Ermon, Denoising Diffusion Implicit Models (2020)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.02502) motivates respaced reverse trajectories. This experiment freezes the preceding DDPM's5504 parameters and adds no training. It compares8,16 and 32 reverse evaluations at eta0 and eta1, using common starting noise and replayable innovation banks.

The source infers x_0, clips it, and recomputes epsilon from the clipped value before constructing the DDIM mean. The previous=-1 endpoint uses alpha_bar_previous=1. Eta0 removes innovation dependence, while eta1 on all 32 adjacent levels agrees with the source DDPM trajectory up to Float32 arithmetic: maximum residual5.468726158e-6. These are transition controls, rather than evidence that fewer evaluations preserve sample quality. Every path uses the same short-fit model and inherits its baseline shortfall.

> Follow the state through one model operation

The method diagram separates the explicitly supplied state and conditioning from the predictor and the single transition. The caller owns repeated transitions and training.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ddim_method.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::Diagram d(28,1150);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle s;s.width=11;s.height=5;s.font_size=30;d.node("input",{-18,0},"Frozen DDPM\nRespaced times",s).node("predict",{-5,0},"Clip inferred x0\nRecompute epsilon",s).node("step",{8,0},"DDIM transition\nExplicit eta/noise",s).node("loop",{21,0},"8 / 16 / 32 steps\nCaller owns path",s).edge("input","predict").edge("predict","step").edge("step","loop").save_svg("build/ddim_method.svg");}
```
